WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:03.110
Film Courage: Since [your book] Writing for
the Green Light which was published in 2014

00:00:03.110 --> 00:00:04.110
or …?

00:00:04.110 --> 00:00:05.110
Scott Kirkpatrick: 2015.

00:00:05.110 --> 00:00:06.230
Film Courage: 2015…okay.

00:00:06.230 --> 00:00:11.030
Has your personal opinion wavered on anything
in the book that you were maybe more dogmatic

00:00:11.030 --> 00:00:15.790
about and then since [such as] a new film
has come out or some thing has shifted, you’ve

00:00:15.790 --> 00:00:16.800
actually changed your opinion?

00:00:16.800 --> 00:00:17.800
Scott Kirkpatrick: No.

00:00:17.800 --> 00:00:24.400
I mean only in the sense that when you’re
writing a book…you know…if it were a blog

00:00:24.400 --> 00:00:28.100
or something else, you can be a little bit
more like this is what is working today and

00:00:28.100 --> 00:00:30.369
right now.

00:00:30.369 --> 00:00:36.900
I wrote that book and the book I am working
on also about distribution, I focus on principles,

00:00:36.900 --> 00:00:40.000
not rules, not gimmicks, not trends, principles.

00:00:40.000 --> 00:00:44.020
Think about engineering for instance.

00:00:44.020 --> 00:00:49.190
We sent human beings to the moon and we returned
them to the earth with as much computing power

00:00:49.190 --> 00:00:51.980
on board as 4-function calculator.

00:00:51.980 --> 00:00:56.320
People were using slide rulers at NASA to
calculate how are we going to land an object

00:00:56.320 --> 00:00:57.690
on the moon.

00:00:57.690 --> 00:01:03.230
So you think technology today is going to
send us to wherever and all these other things.

00:01:03.230 --> 00:01:07.270
The truth is the principles that work in technology
today in an engineering level are exactly

00:01:07.270 --> 00:01:11.550
the same as they were in the 60’s, just
different tools to get to the same result.

00:01:11.550 --> 00:01:12.810
Writing for the Green Light is the same idea.

00:01:12.810 --> 00:01:17.350
If you are trying to be a writer, if you are
trying to build your career as a writer, there

00:01:17.350 --> 00:01:21.321
are certain tools that you have in your tool
kit that are going to propel you and there

00:01:21.321 --> 00:01:23.420
are others that are just going to hold you
back.

00:01:23.420 --> 00:01:28.420
Those Goldmine Genres that I wrote about in
the book are as true today as they were in

00:01:28.420 --> 00:01:33.409
the 60’s, as they were in the 40’s, as
they will be 30 years from now.

00:01:33.409 --> 00:01:37.490
You know, Sharknado is a great example of
a creature feature.

00:01:37.490 --> 00:01:42.710
You see women in peril thrillers all the time
on TV, you see them all over the world.

00:01:42.710 --> 00:01:50.380
Action movies with aging stars have been here
from the beginning of the B-movie, as the

00:01:50.380 --> 00:01:52.250
second-tier to the major one.

00:01:52.250 --> 00:01:53.690
And they are going to continue no matter what.

00:01:53.690 --> 00:01:57.830
They were big in the 80’s and the 90’s
because of video rentals and now they are

00:01:57.830 --> 00:02:00.340
big today because of VOD opportunities.

00:02:00.340 --> 00:02:06.220
These are just core value content pieces that
every company looks for in one capacity or

00:02:06.220 --> 00:02:08.180
another.

00:02:08.180 --> 00:02:14.760
It’s as true as it was written a few years
ago as it will be in the future.

00:02:14.760 --> 00:02:18.510
And because it’s a book, I really focus
on the stuff that is going to stick around.

